1
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:11,679
Kurt Girdle sits at a strange junction

2
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:14,639
in human thought. A mathematician who

3
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:17,920
shattered mathematics. A logician who

4
00:00:14,639 --> 00:00:21,039
proved logic incomplete. A rationalist

5
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:23,519
who believed in God and the afterlife. A

6
00:00:21,039 --> 00:00:27,760
realist about abstract objects who

7
00:00:23,519 --> 00:00:30,400
argued time itself might be ideal.

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:34,079
Born in 1906 in what is now the Czech

9
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:36,719
Republic, dying in 1978 in Princeton,

10
00:00:34,079 --> 00:00:38,800
New Jersey, Girdle produced work that

11
00:00:36,719 --> 00:00:42,079
reverberates through mathematics,

12
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:44,879
physics, philosophy, computer science,

13
00:00:42,079 --> 00:00:47,920
and cognitive science with a force that

14
00:00:44,879 --> 00:00:49,680
has not diminished.

15
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:52,320
Here's what makes Girdle different from

16
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:55,039
most thinkers. He did not write

17
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:58,719
manifestos or build grand philosophical

18
00:00:55,039 --> 00:01:01,520
systems in pros. Instead, he constructed

19
00:00:58,719 --> 00:01:04,400
mathematical proofs that carried

20
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:06,640
philosophical weight. Theorems that

21
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:10,080
forced anyone who understood them to

22
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:15,920
rethink the nature of truth, knowledge,

23
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:19,280
proof, time, and existence itself. His

24
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:21,119
famous incompleteness theorems from 1931

25
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,119
demonstrated something shocking about

26
00:01:21,119 --> 00:01:26,640
formal systems.

27
00:01:23,119 --> 00:01:28,880
His rotating universe solution from 1949

28
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:31,119
showed that Einstein's equations allowed

29
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:34,320
for time travel.

30
00:01:31,119 --> 00:01:36,960
His ontological proof kept private until

31
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:41,439
near death attempted to demonstrate

32
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:43,920
God's existence through pure logic.

33
00:01:41,439 --> 00:01:46,240
But beyond these landmark results,

34
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:47,840
Girdle held a coherent philosophical

35
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:50,399
worldview,

36
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:51,920
what he called rationalistic,

37
00:01:50,399 --> 00:01:55,840
idealistic,

38
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:57,920
optimistic, and theological.

39
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:00,079
He believed in the power of human reason

40
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:03,200
to grasp objective truths about an

41
00:02:00,079 --> 00:02:05,680
abstract realm. He thought mathematics

42
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:09,119
described a real world of concepts that

43
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:11,039
existed independently of human minds.

44
00:02:09,119 --> 00:02:14,000
He was convinced that every meaningful

45
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:16,640
problem has a solution even if current

46
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,480
methods cannot find it.

47
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:21,120
And he believed this commitment to

48
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:25,200
reason and abstract reality pointed

49
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:27,280
towards something beyond the physical.

50
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,120
Understanding Girdle requires grasping

51
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:32,560
how his mathematical work and

52
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:34,400
philosophical views intertwined.

53
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:37,360
The incompleteness theorems were not

54
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:39,920
just technical results. They emerged

55
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,160
from and supported his platonism about

56
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:44,560
mathematics.

57
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:47,680
His work on relativity connected to his

58
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:49,840
views about the nature of time. His

59
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:52,319
ontological proof expressed his

60
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:56,080
rationalist conviction that pure thought

61
00:02:52,319 --> 00:02:59,840
could reach metaphysical truths.

62
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:02,959
Every piece fits into a larger vision.

63
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:05,120
This exploration will unfold in parts,

64
00:03:02,959 --> 00:03:07,599
each examining a major dimension of

65
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:10,239
Girdle's thought.

66
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:12,800
We begin with what made him famous, the

67
00:03:10,239 --> 00:03:14,879
incompleteness theorems.

68
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:17,840
Then we move through his philosophy of

69
00:03:14,879 --> 00:03:21,040
mathematics, his contributions to set

70
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:23,120
theory and logic, his work in physics

71
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:25,440
and cosmology,

72
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:27,440
his engagement with idealism and the

73
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:30,799
nature of time,

74
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:33,120
his ontological argument, and finally

75
00:03:30,799 --> 00:03:37,440
his broader philosophical commitments

76
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:42,400
and influence on leading thinkers.

77
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:45,440
Part one, the incompleteness theorems.

78
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:48,560
In the summer of 1930, Kurt Girdle was

79
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:50,799
24 years old, recently graduated with

80
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:54,239
his doctorate and thinking about the

81
00:03:50,799 --> 00:03:55,840
foundations of mathematics.

82
00:03:54,239 --> 00:03:58,400
The prevailing mood among the

83
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:00,879
mathematical elite was optimistic,

84
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,959
almost triumphant.

85
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:05,680
David Hilbert, perhaps the most

86
00:04:02,959 --> 00:04:09,200
influential mathematician of the era,

87
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:11,760
had proposed an ambitious program.

88
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:14,879
He wanted to put all of mathematics on a

89
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:17,840
secure foundation by finding a complete

90
00:04:14,879 --> 00:04:19,680
and consistent set of axioms from which

91
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:23,520
every mathematical truth could be

92
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:25,919
derived through mechanical rules.

93
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:29,199
The idea was to eliminate doubt,

94
00:04:25,919 --> 00:04:31,680
ambiguity, and paradox from mathematics

95
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:33,759
forever.

96
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:35,280
Many believed this goal was within

97
00:04:33,759 --> 00:04:37,919
reach.

98
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:40,400
Girdle later described how he approached

99
00:04:37,919 --> 00:04:41,840
the problem.

100
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:44,960
He started thinking about the

101
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:46,800
consistency of analysis, a branch of

102
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:50,000
mathematics dealing with continuous

103
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:52,479
functions and calculus.

104
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,120
As he worked on this, he realized he

105
00:04:52,479 --> 00:04:58,160
needed to use the concept of truth for

106
00:04:55,120 --> 00:05:00,479
arithmetic, the basic mathematics of

107
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:05,199
counting and calculation

108
00:05:00,479 --> 00:05:07,840
to verify the axioms of analysis.

109
00:05:05,199 --> 00:05:09,680
This was already strange.

110
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:12,639
The relationship between truth and

111
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:15,520
provability, between what is true and

112
00:05:12,639 --> 00:05:18,400
what can be proven became the focus of

113
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:20,560
his thinking.

114
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:22,080
Within months, Girdle had discovered

115
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:24,560
something that would stun the

116
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:26,880
mathematical world.

117
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:30,560
He announced his results at a conference

118
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:32,960
in Kigburg in September 1930. Though

119
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:36,320
many in attendance did not immediately

120
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:38,800
grasp their significance.

121
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:41,520
John vonomanyman, one of the few who

122
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:44,240
understood, reportedly stayed up all

123
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:46,240
night after hearing Girdle present and

124
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:50,720
within days had worked out the second

125
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:53,520
incompleteness theorem as a corollary.

126
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:55,520
What Girdle proved came in two parts,

127
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:58,479
now called the first and second

128
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:00,639
incompleteness theorems.

129
00:05:58,479 --> 00:06:05,600
Let me explain them without technical

130
00:06:00,639 --> 00:06:07,520
symbols, focusing on what they mean.

131
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:10,720
The first incompleteness theorem says

132
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:12,800
this. Any consistent formal system that

133
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:14,960
is powerful enough to express basic

134
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:16,560
arithmetic will necessarily be

135
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:18,319
incomplete.

136
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,919
Incomplete means there will be

137
00:06:18,319 --> 00:06:22,160
statements in the language of that

138
00:06:19,919 --> 00:06:24,880
system that are true but cannot be

139
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:26,800
proven within the system.

140
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:29,600
No matter how many axioms you start

141
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:32,319
with, no matter how clever your rules of

142
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:35,360
inference, if your system can do basic

143
00:06:32,319 --> 00:06:40,080
arithmetic and is consistent, then it

144
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:42,000
cannot prove all truths about numbers.

145
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:45,520
The second incompleteness theorem goes

146
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,759
further. Such a system cannot prove its

147
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:49,600
own consistency.

148
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:52,240
If your mathematical system is

149
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:54,880
consistent, it cannot prove that fact

150
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:57,039
about itself using only its own rules

151
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:59,759
and axioms.

152
00:06:57,039 --> 00:07:04,639
This means mathematics cannot establish

153
00:06:59,759 --> 00:07:07,360
its own reliability from within.

154
00:07:04,639 --> 00:07:08,960
Now, these statements are profound but

155
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:11,039
abstract.

156
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:14,000
Let me unpack the reasoning behind them

157
00:07:11,039 --> 00:07:15,840
and explain why they matter so deeply.

158
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:19,199
Girdle's proof works through an

159
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:21,599
ingenious self-referential construction.

160
00:07:19,199 --> 00:07:24,960
He showed how to encode statements about

161
00:07:21,599 --> 00:07:27,440
a formal system within that system using

162
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:29,599
the systems own notation.

163
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:32,240
This encoding, now called girdle

164
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:35,199
numbering, assigns a unique number to

165
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:38,160
every symbol, formula, and proof in the

166
00:07:35,199 --> 00:07:40,240
system. Through this numbering,

167
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:42,319
statements about provability can be

168
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:44,160
translated into statements about

169
00:07:42,319 --> 00:07:46,000
arithmetic.

170
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:48,560
This is the technical wizardry that

171
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,479
makes everything work. Girdle found a

172
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,880
way to make mathematics talk about

173
00:07:50,479 --> 00:07:55,440
itself.

174
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:58,879
Using this encoding, Girdle constructed

175
00:07:55,440 --> 00:08:02,160
a specific sentence, call it G, that

176
00:07:58,879 --> 00:08:05,199
asserts its own unprovability.

177
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:07,680
In ordinary language, G says this

178
00:08:05,199 --> 00:08:09,280
sentence cannot be proven in this formal

179
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:12,240
system.

180
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:16,240
Now think about what happens. If the

181
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:18,160
system can prove G, then G is false. But

182
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:21,680
that would mean the system proves false

183
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:24,160
statements making it inconsistent.

184
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:26,000
If the system is consistent, it cannot

185
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:28,479
prove G.

186
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:31,360
But if it cannot prove G, then G is

187
00:08:28,479 --> 00:08:34,640
true. There really is a true statement.

188
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:37,360
The system cannot prove the system is

189
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:39,919
incomplete.

190
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:42,880
This is reminiscent of the liars paradox

191
00:08:39,919 --> 00:08:45,279
where someone says I am lying. But

192
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:47,680
Girdle's construction avoids the paradox

193
00:08:45,279 --> 00:08:49,360
by moving to a mathematical context

194
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:52,399
where we can establish truth

195
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:55,600
independently of provability.

196
00:08:52,399 --> 00:08:57,760
The girdle sentence is not paradoxical.

197
00:08:55,600 --> 00:09:00,240
It is simply a true statement that

198
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:02,160
happens to be unprovable within its own

199
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:04,959
system.

200
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:07,519
The underlying assumptions matter here.

201
00:09:04,959 --> 00:09:10,000
Girdle's proof applies to formal systems

202
00:09:07,519 --> 00:09:11,680
that are consistent and sufficiently

203
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,080
powerful.

204
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:17,200
Consistent means the system never proves

205
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:19,839
both a statement and its negation. It

206
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:22,080
never contradicts itself.

207
00:09:19,839 --> 00:09:24,880
Sufficiently powerful means the system

208
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:27,040
can express basic facts about numbers

209
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:29,040
and perform elementary arithmetic

210
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:31,279
operations.

211
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:34,480
Nearly every foundational system for

212
00:09:31,279 --> 00:09:38,080
mathematics meets these criteria.

213
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:40,800
Piano arithmetic, set theory, any system

214
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:45,600
mathematicians actually use for serious

215
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:47,760
work all fall under Girdle's theorems.

216
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:50,320
One immediate consequence shook the

217
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:53,440
mathematical establishment.

218
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:55,040
Hilbert's program was impossible.

219
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:56,959
There could be no complete and

220
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:58,560
consistent axiomatization of

221
00:09:56,959 --> 00:10:01,200
mathematics.

222
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:04,399
No finite set of axioms and rules could

223
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:06,560
capture all mathematical truth. The

224
00:10:04,399 --> 00:10:08,880
dream of reducing mathematics to a

225
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:11,040
mechanical procedure where every

226
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:14,640
question could be decided by following

227
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:16,640
algorithmic steps was over.

228
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:19,839
Mathematics would always require

229
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:22,480
creativity, insight, and genuinely new

230
00:10:19,839 --> 00:10:24,640
ideas beyond what any fixed system could

231
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:27,040
generate.

232
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:28,560
But the implications spread far beyond

233
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:30,240
the technical foundations of

234
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:32,399
mathematics.

235
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:34,720
Girdle's theorems revealed something

236
00:10:32,399 --> 00:10:37,279
about the relationship between truth and

237
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:40,720
proof, between knowledge and formal

238
00:10:37,279 --> 00:10:44,079
systems, between what exists and what we

239
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:47,680
can establish. Consider what it means

240
00:10:44,079 --> 00:10:49,920
for truth to transcend provability.

241
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:52,240
We can recognize the girdle sentence as

242
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:55,360
true even though the formal system

243
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:57,279
cannot prove it. This recognition

244
00:10:55,360 --> 00:11:00,560
happens through a kind of informal

245
00:10:57,279 --> 00:11:02,399
reasoning that steps outside the system.

246
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:05,839
We use our understanding of what the

247
00:11:02,399 --> 00:11:08,160
system can and cannot do to see that G

248
00:11:05,839 --> 00:11:10,880
must be true.

249
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:12,880
This suggests human mathematical insight

250
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:17,200
involves something that cannot be fully

251
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:19,279
captured by any fixed set of rules.

252
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:22,079
The second incompleteness theorem has

253
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:24,880
its own profound implications.

254
00:11:22,079 --> 00:11:26,959
A mathematical system cannot certify its

255
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:29,920
own consistency.

256
00:11:26,959 --> 00:11:32,000
To prove your axioms are coherent, you

257
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:34,320
need to step outside them and use

258
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,959
stronger assumptions.

259
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:39,040
This creates a kind of hierarchy.

260
00:11:36,959 --> 00:11:42,000
You can prove the consistency of one

261
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:44,320
system by working in a stronger system.

262
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:46,240
But then that stronger system faces the

263
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:49,200
same limitation.

264
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:51,519
There is no ultimate foundation, no

265
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:53,680
bedrock where mathematics can prove its

266
00:11:51,519 --> 00:11:56,000
own coherence without appealing to

267
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:58,320
something beyond itself. Some

268
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:00,560
philosophers and mathematicians saw this

269
00:11:58,320 --> 00:12:02,959
as disastrous.

270
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:04,959
Others found it liberating.

271
00:12:02,959 --> 00:12:07,200
The idea that mathematics has an

272
00:12:04,959 --> 00:12:09,279
inexhaustible depth, that there will

273
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:11,519
always be new truths to discover that

274
00:12:09,279 --> 00:12:13,519
cannot be reached by current methods,

275
00:12:11,519 --> 00:12:16,560
meant the mathematical enterprise would

276
00:12:13,519 --> 00:12:18,639
never become mechanical or complete.

277
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:21,600
Human creativity would always be

278
00:12:18,639 --> 00:12:23,519
necessary.

279
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:25,279
Now, let us examine the relationship

280
00:12:23,519 --> 00:12:28,079
between Girdle's theorems and his

281
00:12:25,279 --> 00:12:31,279
philosophical commitments, particularly

282
00:12:28,079 --> 00:12:33,519
his mathematical platonism.

283
00:12:31,279 --> 00:12:35,839
This connection is crucial but often

284
00:12:33,519 --> 00:12:38,560
misunderstood.

285
00:12:35,839 --> 00:12:41,279
Platonism in mathematics is the view

286
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:43,600
that mathematical objects and truths

287
00:12:41,279 --> 00:12:46,079
exist independently of human minds and

288
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:50,160
formal systems.

289
00:12:46,079 --> 00:12:52,560
Numbers, sets, functions. These are not

290
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:55,680
human inventions or mere symbols we

291
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:58,480
manipulate. They are real entities in an

292
00:12:55,680 --> 00:13:00,800
abstract realm. And mathematics is the

293
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:02,639
science of discovering truths about that

294
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:05,680
realm.

295
00:13:02,639 --> 00:13:08,959
Mathematical truth is objective, not a

296
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:11,120
matter of convention or construction.

297
00:13:08,959 --> 00:13:14,000
Girdle was an unabashed platonist

298
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:16,320
throughout his adult life. He believed

299
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:19,120
mathematics describes a reality as

300
00:13:16,320 --> 00:13:20,959
objective as the physical world, just

301
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:22,639
more abstract.

302
00:13:20,959 --> 00:13:25,200
And his incompleteness theorems

303
00:13:22,639 --> 00:13:27,920
supported this view, at least in his

304
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:29,680
interpretation. Here's

305
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:32,160
the connection.

306
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:34,880
If mathematics were merely about formal

307
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:37,519
systems, if mathematical truth just

308
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:39,839
meant provability within some axiomatic

309
00:13:37,519 --> 00:13:42,079
framework, then the incompleteness

310
00:13:39,839 --> 00:13:44,880
theorems would be puzzling but not

311
00:13:42,079 --> 00:13:46,560
philosophically dramatic. They would

312
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:49,279
simply show that different formal

313
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:52,560
systems have different capabilities and

314
00:13:49,279 --> 00:13:55,200
that is that. But if there is a reality

315
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:58,079
of mathematical truth independent of any

316
00:13:55,200 --> 00:14:00,000
formal system, then the theorems reveal

317
00:13:58,079 --> 00:14:02,800
something profound

318
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:05,360
that this reality outruns what any fixed

319
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:08,000
set of rules can capture.

320
00:14:05,360 --> 00:14:10,079
Truth is bigger than proof. The

321
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,560
mathematical universe has a richness

322
00:14:10,079 --> 00:14:15,920
that cannot be exhausted by any

323
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:18,160
systematic method.

324
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:20,800
Girdle himself made this point explicit

325
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:22,639
in his philosophical writings.

326
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:24,880
He argued that his theorems showed

327
00:14:22,639 --> 00:14:27,199
mathematical truth cannot be reduced to

328
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:29,519
formal provability.

329
00:14:27,199 --> 00:14:32,560
There are arithmetical truths that are

330
00:14:29,519 --> 00:14:34,560
true in an objective sense. Even though

331
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:36,880
particular formal systems cannot

332
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:39,600
establish them,

333
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:41,920
this gap between truth and provability

334
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:44,720
makes sense only if mathematical truth

335
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:47,440
has an objective existence beyond human

336
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:50,880
formal systems.

337
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:52,639
Some anti-platoninists pushed back, "If

338
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:55,519
you do not believe in an independent

339
00:14:52,639 --> 00:14:58,399
realm of mathematical truths, how do you

340
00:14:55,519 --> 00:15:00,639
interpret Girdle's results?"

341
00:14:58,399 --> 00:15:03,040
One response is to stay neutral about

342
00:15:00,639 --> 00:15:05,360
truth and simply say that for any

343
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:07,440
consistent formal system, there are

344
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:09,920
statements formulable in that system

345
00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:11,680
that the system can neither prove nor

346
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:14,160
disprove.

347
00:15:11,680 --> 00:15:15,839
This is undecidability.

348
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:18,399
without any claim about whether the

349
00:15:15,839 --> 00:15:22,160
undecidable statements are really true

350
00:15:18,399 --> 00:15:26,880
or false. The sentence G is undecidable

351
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:28,399
in system S. That is all we can say.

352
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:29,920
But Girdle found this position

353
00:15:28,399 --> 00:15:32,560
untenable.

354
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:34,320
He pointed out that we can establish the

355
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:36,639
truth of the girdle sentence through

356
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:38,959
informal reasoning.

357
00:15:36,639 --> 00:15:42,480
We recognize it as true by understanding

358
00:15:38,959 --> 00:15:44,720
what the formal system can accomplish.

359
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:48,000
This recognition happens in what Girdle

360
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:50,320
called intuition, a faculty of grasping

361
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:54,399
mathematical truths that is not

362
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:56,560
reducible to following mechanical rules.

363
00:15:54,399 --> 00:15:59,839
If our intuition can see truths that

364
00:15:56,560 --> 00:16:02,560
formal systems miss, this suggests we

365
00:15:59,839 --> 00:16:04,480
have access to mathematical reality

366
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:07,199
beyond what any fixed procedure

367
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:10,399
provides.

368
00:16:07,199 --> 00:16:13,120
The debate continues to this day.

369
00:16:10,399 --> 00:16:14,959
Formalists, constructivists, and other

370
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:17,440
anti-realists have developed

371
00:16:14,959 --> 00:16:19,519
sophisticated responses,

372
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:21,519
but Girdle's interpretation remains

373
00:16:19,519 --> 00:16:23,120
influential.

374
00:16:21,519 --> 00:16:25,199
Many working mathematicians are

375
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:28,000
platonists without necessarily

376
00:16:25,199 --> 00:16:30,959
articulating it. They feel they are

377
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:34,240
discovering pre-existing truths, not

378
00:16:30,959 --> 00:16:37,120
inventing arbitrary games.

379
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:40,720
Girdle gave this intuition philosophical

380
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:42,480
and mathematical backing.

381
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:44,399
Now let us look at some specific

382
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:46,560
examples and applications of the

383
00:16:44,399 --> 00:16:48,560
incompleteness theorems to make them

384
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:51,120
more concrete.

385
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:53,920
One famous undecidable statement is the

386
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:56,240
continuum hypothesis.

387
00:16:53,920 --> 00:16:58,240
This concerns the sizes of infinite

388
00:16:56,240 --> 00:17:00,399
sets.

389
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:02,320
Cantor showed there are different sizes

390
00:17:00,399 --> 00:17:05,679
of infinity.

391
00:17:02,320 --> 00:17:09,039
The set of natural numbers 1 2 3 and so

392
00:17:05,679 --> 00:17:12,640
on is infinite. But the set of real

393
00:17:09,039 --> 00:17:15,760
numbers all possible decimal expansions

394
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:18,319
is a bigger infinity.

395
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:20,480
The continuum hypothesis asks whether

396
00:17:18,319 --> 00:17:22,559
there is any size of infinity between

397
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:24,559
these two.

398
00:17:22,559 --> 00:17:26,400
Is there a set larger than the natural

399
00:17:24,559 --> 00:17:27,919
numbers but smaller than the real

400
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:29,760
numbers?

401
00:17:27,919 --> 00:17:33,039
Girdle himself made progress on this

402
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:35,039
question in 1938. by constructing a

403
00:17:33,039 --> 00:17:37,679
mathematical universe called the

404
00:17:35,039 --> 00:17:41,280
constructible universe L where the

405
00:17:37,679 --> 00:17:44,720
continuum hypothesis is true.

406
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:47,120
Paul Cohen later proved in 1963 that

407
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:51,280
there are also mathematical universes

408
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:53,840
where the continuum hypothesis is false.

409
00:17:51,280 --> 00:17:56,559
Together these results show the

410
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:59,919
continuum hypothesis is independent of

411
00:17:56,559 --> 00:18:04,080
the standard axioms of set theory. It is

412
00:17:59,919 --> 00:18:07,200
undecidable in ZFC the zerloan axioms

413
00:18:04,080 --> 00:18:09,360
with the axiom of choice.

414
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:11,840
This is not quite an example of girdles

415
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:14,640
and completeness theorems directly since

416
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:16,720
the continuum hypothesis is about sets

417
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:19,919
rather than arithmetic

418
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:23,120
but it illustrates the same phenomenon.

419
00:18:19,919 --> 00:18:27,280
Fundamental questions can be undecidable

420
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:29,840
within standard foundational systems.

421
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:32,320
Another example is the halting problem

422
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:35,600
from computer science.

423
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:37,840
Alan Turring building on Girdle's work

424
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:40,799
showed that there is no algorithm that

425
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:43,679
can determine for an arbitrary computer

426
00:18:40,799 --> 00:18:46,720
program and input whether the program

427
00:18:43,679 --> 00:18:48,960
will eventually stop running or continue

428
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:51,520
forever.

429
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:53,760
This undecidability result flows from

430
00:18:51,520 --> 00:18:56,240
the same self-referential reasoning

431
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:58,640
Girdle used.

432
00:18:56,240 --> 00:19:00,160
If you could decide the halting problem

433
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:02,960
algorithmically,

434
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:05,760
you could resolve the girdle sentence

435
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:08,160
which is impossible.

436
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:11,679
The halting problem is the computational

437
00:19:08,160 --> 00:19:14,000
analog of incompleteness.

438
00:19:11,679 --> 00:19:16,000
There are also undecidable statements

439
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:19,280
that arise naturally in different

440
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:22,080
branches of mathematics.

441
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:25,280
Goodstein's theorem which makes claims

442
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:30,720
about sequences of natural numbers is

443
00:19:25,280 --> 00:19:33,280
true but unprovable in piano arithmetic.

444
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:36,160
Krisll's tree theorem relevant to

445
00:19:33,280 --> 00:19:38,799
computer science and combinotaurics

446
00:19:36,160 --> 00:19:44,080
is likewise unprovable in standard

447
00:19:38,799 --> 00:19:47,039
systems but provable in stronger ones.

448
00:19:44,080 --> 00:19:49,200
These are not artificial constructions.

449
00:19:47,039 --> 00:19:51,840
There are questions mathematicians

450
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:54,240
actually care about that happen to

451
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:57,120
exceed the proving power of particular

452
00:19:54,240 --> 00:20:00,799
formal systems.

453
00:19:57,120 --> 00:20:03,679
The philosophical upshot is this.

454
00:20:00,799 --> 00:20:06,640
Incompleteness is not a quirk or defect

455
00:20:03,679 --> 00:20:09,120
in our foundational systems.

456
00:20:06,640 --> 00:20:11,919
It is an intrinsic feature of how

457
00:20:09,120 --> 00:20:14,799
mathematics works.

458
00:20:11,919 --> 00:20:19,039
Any consistent system powerful enough to

459
00:20:14,799 --> 00:20:22,799
do serious work will have blind spots,

460
00:20:19,039 --> 00:20:24,400
truths it cannot reach.

461
00:20:22,799 --> 00:20:27,360
This affects how we understand

462
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:28,960
mathematical knowledge, the limits of

463
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:31,280
computation

464
00:20:28,960 --> 00:20:33,919
and the nature of formal reasoning

465
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:36,159
itself.

466
00:20:33,919 --> 00:20:37,919
Let me turn now to some major critiques

467
00:20:36,159 --> 00:20:40,480
and debates surrounding the

468
00:20:37,919 --> 00:20:42,240
incompleteness theorems.

469
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:44,720
Not everyone accepts Girdle's

470
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:48,080
interpretation or agrees about the

471
00:20:44,720 --> 00:20:50,799
significance of his results.

472
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:53,440
One line of criticism comes from strict

473
00:20:50,799 --> 00:20:55,840
formalists who reject Plleonism

474
00:20:53,440 --> 00:20:58,720
entirely.

475
00:20:55,840 --> 00:21:00,880
They argue that mathematics is just the

476
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:03,200
study of formal systems and their

477
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:07,360
consequences.

478
00:21:03,200 --> 00:21:10,240
Truth is definable only within a system.

479
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:14,240
It makes no sense to say a statement is

480
00:21:10,240 --> 00:21:17,120
really true but unprovable.

481
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:20,480
For these thinkers, Girdle simply showed

482
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:23,120
that no single formal system exhausts

483
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:26,720
mathematics.

484
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:29,760
We can always move to stronger systems.

485
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:32,080
Mathematics is an open-ended activity of

486
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:34,960
constructing and exploring different

487
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:37,200
formal structures.

488
00:21:34,960 --> 00:21:40,640
Incompleteness is not about truth

489
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:43,280
escaping proof, but about the diversity

490
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:46,280
and richness of possible mathematical

491
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:46,280
frameworks.

492
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:53,760
This formalist response has some merit,

493
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:57,200
but Girdle thought it missed the point.

494
00:21:53,760 --> 00:22:00,240
If we are just playing formal games, why

495
00:21:57,200 --> 00:22:02,880
do mathematicians care so deeply about

496
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:05,440
consistency?

497
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:09,600
Why does it matter that ZFC is

498
00:22:05,440 --> 00:22:12,720
consistent rather than inconsistent?

499
00:22:09,600 --> 00:22:15,919
The answer seems to be that consistency

500
00:22:12,720 --> 00:22:18,400
tracks something real.

501
00:22:15,919 --> 00:22:21,440
Whether the axioms describe a coherent

502
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:24,880
mathematical universe.

503
00:22:21,440 --> 00:22:27,360
This points back toward realism.

504
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:31,200
Another debate concerns whether Girdle's

505
00:22:27,360 --> 00:22:34,799
results apply to the human mind.

506
00:22:31,200 --> 00:22:37,520
Some philosophers, notably J.R. Lucas

507
00:22:34,799 --> 00:22:40,159
and later Roger Penrose,

508
00:22:37,520 --> 00:22:44,960
argued that incompleteness shows human

509
00:22:40,159 --> 00:22:48,559
minds cannot be computational systems.

510
00:22:44,960 --> 00:22:51,280
The reasoning goes like this.

511
00:22:48,559 --> 00:22:54,400
Any computer is equivalent to a touring

512
00:22:51,280 --> 00:22:56,480
machine which is equivalent to a formal

513
00:22:54,400 --> 00:23:00,320
system.

514
00:22:56,480 --> 00:23:03,840
If human minds were formal systems, they

515
00:23:00,320 --> 00:23:06,880
would be subject to Girdle's theorems.

516
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:09,120
But humans can recognize the truth of

517
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:12,559
Girdle sentences that exceed any

518
00:23:09,120 --> 00:23:15,760
particular formal system.

519
00:23:12,559 --> 00:23:17,840
Therefore, human minds are not formal

520
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:20,080
systems.

521
00:23:17,840 --> 00:23:22,960
Therefore, strong artificial

522
00:23:20,080 --> 00:23:25,760
intelligence is impossible.

523
00:23:22,960 --> 00:23:28,000
and human cognition involves something

524
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:30,799
non-algorithmic

525
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:33,120
perhaps quantum mechanical processes in

526
00:23:30,799 --> 00:23:36,000
the brain. This argument has generated

527
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:38,880
enormous controversy. The main objection

528
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:41,360
is that it confuses two things. What a

529
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:43,440
formal system can prove from within and

530
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:45,360
what we can establish about the system

531
00:23:43,440 --> 00:23:47,440
from outside.

532
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:50,000
When we recognize a godal sentence as

533
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:52,720
true, we are reasoning informally about

534
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:56,080
what a formal system can do. we are not

535
00:23:52,720 --> 00:23:58,240
operating within the system.

536
00:23:56,080 --> 00:24:00,799
A sufficiently sophisticated computer

537
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:02,799
could also reason about other computers

538
00:24:00,799 --> 00:24:04,960
and recognize when they cannot prove

539
00:24:02,799 --> 00:24:06,960
certain statements.

540
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:09,760
The fact that we can see truths about

541
00:24:06,960 --> 00:24:12,640
formal systems does not prove we are not

542
00:24:09,760 --> 00:24:16,240
formal systems ourselves. It just shows

543
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:17,840
we can reason at multiple levels.

544
00:24:16,240 --> 00:24:20,400
Penrose responded that human

545
00:24:17,840 --> 00:24:22,799
mathematical intuition involves a direct

546
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:24,480
grasping of truths that cannot be

547
00:24:22,799 --> 00:24:26,720
algorithmic.

548
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:29,200
We perceive mathematical reality through

549
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:30,799
a faculty that is not reducible to rule

550
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:32,880
following.

551
00:24:30,799 --> 00:24:35,760
This perception might involve quantum

552
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:38,080
processes in neural microtubules that

553
00:24:35,760 --> 00:24:40,159
collapse wave functions in ways relevant

554
00:24:38,080 --> 00:24:41,600
to consciousness.

555
00:24:40,159 --> 00:24:44,080
But most philosophers and

556
00:24:41,600 --> 00:24:46,240
neuroscientists find this speculative

557
00:24:44,080 --> 00:24:48,000
and unnecessary.

558
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:49,840
There is no evidence that quantum

559
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:52,400
effects play a role in ordinary

560
00:24:49,840 --> 00:24:55,120
cognition. And the jump from God's

561
00:24:52,400 --> 00:24:58,640
theorems to quantum consciousness seems

562
00:24:55,120 --> 00:25:00,880
to involve several nonsequittors.

563
00:24:58,640 --> 00:25:02,720
What did God himself think about minds

564
00:25:00,880 --> 00:25:05,520
and machines?

565
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:07,279
This is fascinating. He was skeptical

566
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:09,679
that human reason could be fully

567
00:25:07,279 --> 00:25:12,720
mechanized, but not for the reasons

568
00:25:09,679 --> 00:25:14,400
Lucas and Penrose suggested.

569
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:16,799
Goodel thought human mathematical

570
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:19,360
intuition gave access to objective

571
00:25:16,799 --> 00:25:22,000
truths that went beyond any formal

572
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:24,480
system. This intuition was not

573
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:27,360
algorithmic, but it was also not

574
00:25:24,480 --> 00:25:29,760
mysterious or quantum mechanical.

575
00:25:27,360 --> 00:25:32,880
It was a rational faculty for grasping

576
00:25:29,760 --> 00:25:36,080
concepts and their relationships.

577
00:25:32,880 --> 00:25:38,240
The power of this faculty in God's view

578
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:40,799
made it unlikely that we are equivalent

579
00:25:38,240 --> 00:25:43,440
to touring machines. But he never

580
00:25:40,799 --> 00:25:45,760
claimed to have a proof of this. It was

581
00:25:43,440 --> 00:25:49,279
more a philosophical conviction based on

582
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:50,720
his platonism and rationalism.

583
00:25:49,279 --> 00:25:53,520
There are other philosophical

584
00:25:50,720 --> 00:25:55,679
ramifications worth exploring.

585
00:25:53,520 --> 00:25:58,400
Incompleteness has been invoked,

586
00:25:55,679 --> 00:26:00,240
sometimes correctly and sometimes not,

587
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:02,880
in discussions about the limits of

588
00:26:00,240 --> 00:26:05,440
science, the nature of truth in ethics

589
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:09,520
and metaphysics, the foundations of

590
00:26:05,440 --> 00:26:11,760
legal reasoning, and even theology.

591
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:13,679
Let me separate legitimate applications

592
00:26:11,760 --> 00:26:16,640
from overreach.

593
00:26:13,679 --> 00:26:19,200
In science, incompleteness suggests that

594
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:21,440
no finite set of physical laws can

595
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:24,159
entail every true statement about the

596
00:26:21,440 --> 00:26:26,559
universe. If we assume the universe

597
00:26:24,159 --> 00:26:28,480
includes structures capable of encoding

598
00:26:26,559 --> 00:26:30,559
arithmetic,

599
00:26:28,480 --> 00:26:32,799
this is speculative.

600
00:26:30,559 --> 00:26:35,520
Physical reality might not work like a

601
00:26:32,799 --> 00:26:37,679
formal system. But if the laws of

602
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:40,640
physics can be modeled mathematically in

603
00:26:37,679 --> 00:26:42,640
a sufficiently rich way, there could be

604
00:26:40,640 --> 00:26:44,240
questions about the physical world that

605
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:46,159
cannot be settled by the laws

606
00:26:44,240 --> 00:26:48,320
themselves.

607
00:26:46,159 --> 00:26:51,679
Some physicists have noted that certain

608
00:26:48,320 --> 00:26:55,279
problems in quantum gravity or cosmology

609
00:26:51,679 --> 00:26:57,360
might be formally undecidable.

610
00:26:55,279 --> 00:27:00,000
In ethics and metaphysics, the

611
00:26:57,360 --> 00:27:01,919
application is more dubious.

612
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:05,200
Some have argued that incompleteness

613
00:27:01,919 --> 00:27:07,360
shows moral truth cannot be systematized

614
00:27:05,200 --> 00:27:10,000
or that metaphysical questions have no

615
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:12,159
definitive answers.

616
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:15,120
But God's theorems apply to formal

617
00:27:12,159 --> 00:27:16,640
systems that include arithmetic.

618
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:19,760
Moral reasoning might not be

619
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:22,960
formalizable in the relevant sense. And

620
00:27:19,760 --> 00:27:26,000
even if it were, undecidability within a

621
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:27,679
system does not mean there is no truth,

622
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:29,600
just that the truth might require

623
00:27:27,679 --> 00:27:32,080
resources beyond the system to

624
00:27:29,600 --> 00:27:34,159
establish.

625
00:27:32,080 --> 00:27:36,559
Goodel himself believed strongly in

626
00:27:34,159 --> 00:27:39,120
objective truth and thought most

627
00:27:36,559 --> 00:27:40,880
important questions had answers even

628
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:42,960
when current methods could not find

629
00:27:40,880 --> 00:27:45,840
them.

630
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:48,400
In theology, God's work has been invoked

631
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:49,919
both to support and undermine religious

632
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:52,080
belief.

633
00:27:49,919 --> 00:27:53,600
Some argue that incompleteness shows

634
00:27:52,080 --> 00:27:55,200
rational thought cannot be

635
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:57,200
self-sufficient,

636
00:27:55,200 --> 00:27:59,360
pointing toward the need for revelation

637
00:27:57,200 --> 00:28:01,600
or faith.

638
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:04,159
Others say it demonstrates the limits of

639
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:06,799
formal theology.

640
00:28:04,159 --> 00:28:09,440
Godel himself was a theist with complex

641
00:28:06,799 --> 00:28:11,440
religious views, but he did not think

642
00:28:09,440 --> 00:28:14,960
his incompleteness theorems directly

643
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:16,720
implied anything about God's existence.

644
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:20,320
For that, he developed a separate

645
00:28:16,720 --> 00:28:22,080
argument we will explore later.

646
00:28:20,320 --> 00:28:23,679
One particularly misguided

647
00:28:22,080 --> 00:28:26,640
interpretation claims that

648
00:28:23,679 --> 00:28:29,760
incompleteness means anything goes or

649
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:32,240
that there are no objective truths.

650
00:28:29,760 --> 00:28:34,640
This gets it backwards.

651
00:28:32,240 --> 00:28:37,760
Godel showed that truth exceeds what any

652
00:28:34,640 --> 00:28:39,840
fixed formal system can prove.

653
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:42,320
This distinction makes sense only if we

654
00:28:39,840 --> 00:28:43,919
assume there is objective truth to begin

655
00:28:42,320 --> 00:28:46,000
with.

656
00:28:43,919 --> 00:28:48,720
Incompleteness is not about the absence

657
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:53,880
of truth but about the inability of

658
00:28:48,720 --> 00:28:53,880
systematic methods to capture all of it.

659
00:28:54,159 --> 00:28:59,039
Let me address one more subtle issue.

660
00:28:57,120 --> 00:29:01,200
The philosophical question of whether

661
00:28:59,039 --> 00:29:02,799
there exist absolutely undecidable

662
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:04,880
statements.

663
00:29:02,799 --> 00:29:06,880
An absolutely undecidable statement

664
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:09,360
would be one whose truth or falsity

665
00:29:06,880 --> 00:29:11,200
cannot be determined by any means formal

666
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:13,360
or informal.

667
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:15,039
Girdle's theorems establish relative

668
00:29:13,360 --> 00:29:17,279
undecidability.

669
00:29:15,039 --> 00:29:19,120
Statements undecidable in particular

670
00:29:17,279 --> 00:29:20,880
systems.

671
00:29:19,120 --> 00:29:24,159
But could there be statements with no

672
00:29:20,880 --> 00:29:26,960
determinant truth value whatsoever?

673
00:29:24,159 --> 00:29:29,120
Girdle rejected this possibility. As a

674
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:31,039
platonist, he believed every well-formed

675
00:29:29,120 --> 00:29:33,520
mathematical statement has a definite

676
00:29:31,039 --> 00:29:36,880
truth value, even if we never discover

677
00:29:33,520 --> 00:29:38,880
it. The girdle sentence is true, not

678
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:41,440
somehow indeterminate.

679
00:29:38,880 --> 00:29:44,159
The continuum hypothesis is either true

680
00:29:41,440 --> 00:29:47,520
or false in the mathematical universe,

681
00:29:44,159 --> 00:29:49,600
even though it is independent of ZFC.

682
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:52,080
We might not know which, but there is a

683
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:55,039
fact of the matter.

684
00:29:52,080 --> 00:29:57,200
Others disagree. Intuitionists and

685
00:29:55,039 --> 00:30:00,480
constructivists argue that mathematical

686
00:29:57,200 --> 00:30:02,399
truth depends on constructive proof. A

687
00:30:00,480 --> 00:30:05,440
statement is true only if we have a

688
00:30:02,399 --> 00:30:08,480
method of establishing it, false only if

689
00:30:05,440 --> 00:30:12,159
we can refute it, and otherwise neither

690
00:30:08,480 --> 00:30:14,320
true nor false. From this perspective,

691
00:30:12,159 --> 00:30:18,159
absolutely undecidable statements would

692
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:20,080
be meaningless, lacking any truth value.

693
00:30:18,159 --> 00:30:21,440
The debate touches on fundamental

694
00:30:20,080 --> 00:30:24,480
questions in the philosophy of

695
00:30:21,440 --> 00:30:26,559
mathematics about what truth means and

696
00:30:24,480 --> 00:30:29,279
whether it can exist independently of

697
00:30:26,559 --> 00:30:31,120
proof or knowledge. The impact of

698
00:30:29,279 --> 00:30:33,200
Girdle's incompleteness theorems on

699
00:30:31,120 --> 00:30:34,799
mathematical practice has been less

700
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:36,640
dramatic than their philosophical

701
00:30:34,799 --> 00:30:38,640
implications.

702
00:30:36,640 --> 00:30:40,320
Most working mathematicians go about

703
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:42,880
their business without worrying much

704
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:45,120
about undecidable statements.

705
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:47,840
In practice, the undecidable statements

706
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:50,960
that arise naturally tend to be exotic

707
00:30:47,840 --> 00:30:53,279
or concern foundational issues.

708
00:30:50,960 --> 00:30:57,039
Ordinary mathematical questions usually

709
00:30:53,279 --> 00:30:58,399
can be settled within standard systems.

710
00:30:57,039 --> 00:30:59,840
But the theorems changed how

711
00:30:58,399 --> 00:31:02,399
mathematicians think about the

712
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:04,080
foundations of their discipline.

713
00:31:02,399 --> 00:31:06,240
There's a background awareness that

714
00:31:04,080 --> 00:31:08,799
mathematics rests on assumptions that

715
00:31:06,240 --> 00:31:10,880
cannot be fully justified from within

716
00:31:08,799 --> 00:31:12,960
and that the mathematical universe is

717
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:14,880
inexhaustible.

718
00:31:12,960 --> 00:31:16,320
The incompleteness theorems also

719
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:19,200
influenced the development of

720
00:31:16,320 --> 00:31:22,640
mathematical logic, set theory, and

721
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:24,720
computer science in concrete ways. They

722
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:26,559
led to the theory of recursive functions

723
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:28,799
and computability.

724
00:31:26,559 --> 00:31:30,559
They motivated investigations into

725
00:31:28,799 --> 00:31:32,799
different axiom systems and their

726
00:31:30,559 --> 00:31:35,039
relative strength.

727
00:31:32,799 --> 00:31:37,760
They prompted questions about what can

728
00:31:35,039 --> 00:31:40,000
and cannot be computed, algorithmic

729
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:43,279
information theory, and the foundations

730
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:45,679
of artificial intelligence.

731
00:31:43,279 --> 00:31:47,760
Girdle's work opened entire fields of

732
00:31:45,679 --> 00:31:49,840
inquiry.

733
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:51,279
Finally, there's the question of whether

734
00:31:49,840 --> 00:31:52,880
we have fully understood the

735
00:31:51,279 --> 00:31:54,880
philosophical significance of

736
00:31:52,880 --> 00:31:57,519
incompleteness.

737
00:31:54,880 --> 00:31:59,919
Three generations after Girdle's proof,

738
00:31:57,519 --> 00:32:02,320
philosophers and mathematicians continue

739
00:31:59,919 --> 00:32:04,080
to debate what it means.

740
00:32:02,320 --> 00:32:06,880
Does it show that human reason has

741
00:32:04,080 --> 00:32:08,559
limits or that it has inexhaustible

742
00:32:06,880 --> 00:32:11,279
power?

743
00:32:08,559 --> 00:32:14,159
Does it support platonism or undermine

744
00:32:11,279 --> 00:32:15,679
it? Does it reveal something about

745
00:32:14,159 --> 00:32:17,919
consciousness

746
00:32:15,679 --> 00:32:20,720
or is it purely a technical result in

747
00:32:17,919 --> 00:32:23,760
mathematical logic?

748
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:25,919
These questions remain open testimony to

749
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:29,919
the depth and lasting impact of what

750
00:32:25,919 --> 00:32:33,120
Girdle discovered in 1930.

751
00:32:29,919 --> 00:32:36,000
Part two, mathematical platonism and the

752
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:39,519
reality of concepts.

753
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:42,320
Kurt Girdle believed that numbers exist

754
00:32:39,519 --> 00:32:45,519
not as physical objects you can touch,

755
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:48,880
not as neurons firing in human brains,

756
00:32:45,519 --> 00:32:51,200
not as symbols on paper, but as real

757
00:32:48,880 --> 00:32:53,279
entities in an abstract realm with

758
00:32:51,200 --> 00:32:56,320
objective properties independent of

759
00:32:53,279 --> 00:32:58,240
anyone's thoughts about them.

760
00:32:56,320 --> 00:33:01,600
This position called mathematical

761
00:32:58,240 --> 00:33:04,640
platonism or realism was the bedrock of

762
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:05,840
his entire philosophical outlook.

763
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:09,120
Everything else he thought about

764
00:33:05,840 --> 00:33:12,080
mathematics, logic, knowledge, and truth

765
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:13,519
flowed from this conviction.

766
00:33:12,080 --> 00:33:17,039
But what does it mean to say that

767
00:33:13,519 --> 00:33:19,440
numbers or sets exist when they clearly

768
00:33:17,039 --> 00:33:21,200
are not physical?

769
00:33:19,440 --> 00:33:23,760
How could we possibly know anything

770
00:33:21,200 --> 00:33:26,080
about objects that do not exist in space

771
00:33:23,760 --> 00:33:28,320
or time?

772
00:33:26,080 --> 00:33:31,120
And if mathematics is about discovering

773
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:33,279
truths in an independent realm, what

774
00:33:31,120 --> 00:33:35,440
faculty allows us to perceive these

775
00:33:33,279 --> 00:33:37,440
truths?

776
00:33:35,440 --> 00:33:39,840
These questions obsessed Girdle

777
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:42,240
throughout his life, and his answers

778
00:33:39,840 --> 00:33:44,559
reveal a sophisticated philosophical

779
00:33:42,240 --> 00:33:46,480
position that goes far beyond naive

780
00:33:44,559 --> 00:33:48,240
Plonism.

781
00:33:46,480 --> 00:33:50,399
Let me start with what Girdle actually

782
00:33:48,240 --> 00:33:52,399
meant by platonism.

783
00:33:50,399 --> 00:33:55,120
He defined it carefully in his published

784
00:33:52,399 --> 00:33:58,080
work and conversations.

785
00:33:55,120 --> 00:34:00,640
In his 1951 Gibbs lecture, he

786
00:33:58,080 --> 00:34:02,960
characterized realism or platonism as

787
00:34:00,640 --> 00:34:05,360
the view that mathematical objects and

788
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:07,679
facts exist objectively and

789
00:34:05,360 --> 00:34:10,240
independently of our mental acts and

790
00:34:07,679 --> 00:34:12,560
decisions.

791
00:34:10,240 --> 00:34:16,399
Mathematical truth is not created by

792
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:18,560
mathematicians. It is discovered

793
00:34:16,399 --> 00:34:20,639
When we prove the Pythagorean theorem,

794
00:34:18,560 --> 00:34:23,520
we are not inventing a relationship

795
00:34:20,639 --> 00:34:25,760
between the sides of right triangles. We

796
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:28,159
are uncovering a truth that held before

797
00:34:25,760 --> 00:34:30,639
humans existed and would continue to

798
00:34:28,159 --> 00:34:33,639
hold even if all minds vanished from the

799
00:34:30,639 --> 00:34:33,639
universe.

800
00:34:34,240 --> 00:34:38,320
This might sound obvious to working

801
00:34:35,919 --> 00:34:40,079
mathematicians, many of whom operate as

802
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:42,720
if they are exploring a pre-existing

803
00:34:40,079 --> 00:34:44,240
landscape, but it is philosophically

804
00:34:42,720 --> 00:34:45,919
controversial.

805
00:34:44,240 --> 00:34:48,480
The main alternative views are

806
00:34:45,919 --> 00:34:50,800
formalism, which treats mathematics as

807
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:52,639
the study of formal symbol systems

808
00:34:50,800 --> 00:34:55,200
without reference to any external

809
00:34:52,639 --> 00:34:57,280
reality, and constructivism or

810
00:34:55,200 --> 00:34:58,960
intuitionism, which holds that

811
00:34:57,280 --> 00:35:01,760
mathematical objects are mental

812
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:04,079
constructions, and mathematical truth

813
00:35:01,760 --> 00:35:06,240
depends on proof or construction by

814
00:35:04,079 --> 00:35:09,280
mathematicians.

815
00:35:06,240 --> 00:35:11,599
Girdle rejected both alternatives.

816
00:35:09,280 --> 00:35:13,839
against formalism. He pointed out that

817
00:35:11,599 --> 00:35:16,320
mathematicians care about consistency

818
00:35:13,839 --> 00:35:18,000
and truth, not just syntactic

819
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:21,040
manipulation.

820
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:22,560
We do not choose axioms arbitrarily. We

821
00:35:21,040 --> 00:35:24,320
select them because we believe they

822
00:35:22,560 --> 00:35:26,320
accurately describe mathematical

823
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:28,800
reality.

824
00:35:26,320 --> 00:35:30,960
Against constructivism, he argued that

825
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:33,760
limiting mathematical truth to what we

826
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:36,400
can construct or prove is arbitrary and

827
00:35:33,760 --> 00:35:39,119
restrictive. There are infinitely many

828
00:35:36,400 --> 00:35:41,920
mathematical truths we will never prove.

829
00:35:39,119 --> 00:35:44,320
Does that make them not true?

830
00:35:41,920 --> 00:35:46,880
Girdle thought this was absurd. His

831
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:49,920
platonism extended beyond objects to

832
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:52,640
concepts. This is crucial and often

833
00:35:49,920 --> 00:35:55,760
overlooked. Girdle was not just claiming

834
00:35:52,640 --> 00:35:57,920
that individual numbers or sets exist.

835
00:35:55,760 --> 00:36:01,040
He believed that mathematical concepts

836
00:35:57,920 --> 00:36:04,160
have objective reality. The concept of

837
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:06,720
set, the concept of number, the concept

838
00:36:04,160 --> 00:36:09,119
of function. These are not arbitrary

839
00:36:06,720 --> 00:36:11,200
human inventions but objective features

840
00:36:09,119 --> 00:36:13,680
of reality that we grasp through

841
00:36:11,200 --> 00:36:16,000
intuition.

842
00:36:13,680 --> 00:36:18,400
This focus on concepts rather than just

843
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:21,040
objects gave Girdle's platonism a

844
00:36:18,400 --> 00:36:23,680
distinctive character. It connected to

845
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:27,359
his engagement with phenomenology which

846
00:36:23,680 --> 00:36:29,359
we will explore later. For now, note

847
00:36:27,359 --> 00:36:32,560
that concepts are in some sense more

848
00:36:29,359 --> 00:36:34,560
fundamental than particular objects.

849
00:36:32,560 --> 00:36:37,440
Understanding the concept of set allows

850
00:36:34,560 --> 00:36:39,920
us to reason about all sets, not just

851
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:41,760
particular examples.

852
00:36:39,920 --> 00:36:45,599
Mathematical knowledge proceeds by

853
00:36:41,760 --> 00:36:47,280
clarifying and analyzing concepts.

854
00:36:45,599 --> 00:36:50,320
Now, let us examine Girdle's argument

855
00:36:47,280 --> 00:36:52,560
for Pltonism. He did not simply assert

856
00:36:50,320 --> 00:36:56,000
it. He tried to provide rational

857
00:36:52,560 --> 00:36:58,079
justification. His main argument appears

858
00:36:56,000 --> 00:37:01,119
in the Gibbs lecture and in his papers

859
00:36:58,079 --> 00:37:04,560
on set theory from the 1940s.

860
00:37:01,119 --> 00:37:07,839
The argument runs roughly like this.

861
00:37:04,560 --> 00:37:10,720
Consider how mathematics actually works.

862
00:37:07,839 --> 00:37:13,680
Mathematicians propose axioms, derive

863
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:16,400
theorems and develop theories. But they

864
00:37:13,680 --> 00:37:19,200
do not choose axioms capriciously.

865
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:21,119
They select axioms that seem evident

866
00:37:19,200 --> 00:37:24,320
that capture our intuitive understanding

867
00:37:21,119 --> 00:37:26,880
of the mathematical domain in question.

868
00:37:24,320 --> 00:37:29,119
For example, the axioms of set theory

869
00:37:26,880 --> 00:37:31,200
codify our intuitive concept of

870
00:37:29,119 --> 00:37:32,880
collection.

871
00:37:31,200 --> 00:37:35,280
When mathematicians discover that

872
00:37:32,880 --> 00:37:39,119
certain axioms lead to paradoxes or

873
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:41,359
contradictions, they revise them. This

874
00:37:39,119 --> 00:37:43,680
process makes sense only if there is an

875
00:37:41,359 --> 00:37:45,440
objective reality the axioms are trying

876
00:37:43,680 --> 00:37:47,680
to capture.

877
00:37:45,440 --> 00:37:49,520
We revise our axioms when they fail to

878
00:37:47,680 --> 00:37:52,079
describe mathematical reality

879
00:37:49,520 --> 00:37:54,320
accurately.

880
00:37:52,079 --> 00:37:57,760
Furthermore, consider how mathematical

881
00:37:54,320 --> 00:37:59,920
concepts get clarified over time. The

882
00:37:57,760 --> 00:38:01,920
concept of number, for instance, was

883
00:37:59,920 --> 00:38:03,680
initially vague.

884
00:38:01,920 --> 00:38:06,800
Through mathematical work spanning

885
00:38:03,680 --> 00:38:09,280
centuries, it became precise.

886
00:38:06,800 --> 00:38:12,320
We now understand natural numbers,

887
00:38:09,280 --> 00:38:15,760
integers, rational numbers, real

888
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:17,839
numbers, complex numbers. Each a

889
00:38:15,760 --> 00:38:20,320
clarification and extension of the

890
00:38:17,839 --> 00:38:22,320
initial concept.

891
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:24,400
This process resembles scientific

892
00:38:22,320 --> 00:38:26,240
investigation more than arbitrary

893
00:38:24,400 --> 00:38:27,920
invention.

894
00:38:26,240 --> 00:38:30,560
Mathematicians are exploring a

895
00:38:27,920 --> 00:38:33,520
territory, gradually mapping it with

896
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:35,040
increasing precision.

897
00:38:33,520 --> 00:38:37,839
Girdle also appealed to the

898
00:38:35,040 --> 00:38:39,680
applicability of mathematics.

899
00:38:37,839 --> 00:38:42,160
Mathematical structures discovered

900
00:38:39,680 --> 00:38:44,079
purely for their intrinsic interest

901
00:38:42,160 --> 00:38:47,920
often turn out to describe physical

902
00:38:44,079 --> 00:38:50,560
reality with uncanny accuracy.

903
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:53,280
Non- uklitian geometry developed as a

904
00:38:50,560 --> 00:38:56,400
purely mathematical exercise became

905
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:59,119
essential for general relativity.

906
00:38:56,400 --> 00:39:01,920
Complex numbers initially considered

907
00:38:59,119 --> 00:39:04,320
bizarre fictions are indispensable in

908
00:39:01,920 --> 00:39:06,560
quantum mechanics.

909
00:39:04,320 --> 00:39:08,800
This suggests mathematics is not just a

910
00:39:06,560 --> 00:39:11,359
game we play but a description of

911
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:14,400
objective structures that happen to be

912
00:39:11,359 --> 00:39:16,400
realized in nature.

913
00:39:14,400 --> 00:39:18,320
The incompleteness theorems provided

914
00:39:16,400 --> 00:39:19,920
another argument.

915
00:39:18,320 --> 00:39:22,079
We have already seen how Girdle

916
00:39:19,920 --> 00:39:24,960
interpreted them as showing that truth

917
00:39:22,079 --> 00:39:27,119
exceeds provability.

918
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:30,640
Mathematical truth cannot be reduced to

919
00:39:27,119 --> 00:39:33,359
what any formal system can establish.

920
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:35,599
This makes sense if truth is objective

921
00:39:33,359 --> 00:39:38,800
existing independently of our methods of

922
00:39:35,599 --> 00:39:41,040
discovering it. If mathematical truth

923
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:43,200
were just whatever we can prove,

924
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:46,320
incompleteness would mean mathematics is

925
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:48,400
infected with indeterminacy.

926
00:39:46,320 --> 00:39:50,240
But if truth is objective,

927
00:39:48,400 --> 00:39:52,960
incompleteness just shows our methods

928
00:39:50,240 --> 00:39:56,079
are limited, not that truth itself is

929
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:58,480
incomplete. Despite these arguments, God

930
00:39:56,079 --> 00:40:01,839
admitted he could not prove platonism in

931
00:39:58,480 --> 00:40:04,320
any decisive way. In his conversations

932
00:40:01,839 --> 00:40:07,200
with the philosopher How Wang, he

933
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:09,520
expressed frustration about this. The

934
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:12,480
best he could do, he thought, was to

935
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:15,280
enumerate all the alternative positions,

936
00:40:12,480 --> 00:40:18,000
formalism, constructivism,

937
00:40:15,280 --> 00:40:20,400
nominalism, and show why each was

938
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:23,119
inadequate.

939
00:40:20,400 --> 00:40:26,079
If you eliminate every alternative, what

940
00:40:23,119 --> 00:40:28,000
remains must be correct. But this

941
00:40:26,079 --> 00:40:30,400
dialectical approach never fully

942
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:32,480
satisfied him because he could not be

943
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:34,960
certain he had considered every possible

944
00:40:32,480 --> 00:40:36,560
alternative.

945
00:40:34,960 --> 00:40:38,079
This brings us to mathematical

946
00:40:36,560 --> 00:40:40,079
intuition.

947
00:40:38,079 --> 00:40:42,480
Perhaps the most controversial aspect of

948
00:40:40,079 --> 00:40:45,760
God's philosophy.

949
00:40:42,480 --> 00:40:48,800
If abstract mathematical objects exist,

950
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:52,079
how do we know about them? We cannot see

951
00:40:48,800 --> 00:40:55,440
numbers or touch sets. We cannot perform

952
00:40:52,079 --> 00:40:57,599
experiments on mathematical concepts.

953
00:40:55,440 --> 00:41:00,640
Yet mathematicians have knowledge of

954
00:40:57,599 --> 00:41:03,359
this abstract realm.

955
00:41:00,640 --> 00:41:06,079
Goodel's answer was intuition.

956
00:41:03,359 --> 00:41:09,599
a faculty of directly perceiving or

957
00:41:06,079 --> 00:41:11,599
grasping mathematical truth.

958
00:41:09,599 --> 00:41:15,200
But intuition sounds dangerously

959
00:41:11,599 --> 00:41:18,800
mystical. It evokes images of special

960
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:21,920
powers, ineffable insights, or even

961
00:41:18,800 --> 00:41:24,560
supernatural revelation.

962
00:41:21,920 --> 00:41:26,240
Good was aware of this problem and tried

963
00:41:24,560 --> 00:41:28,319
to make his notion of intuition

964
00:41:26,240 --> 00:41:30,319
respectable and naturalistically

965
00:41:28,319 --> 00:41:32,079
acceptable.

966
00:41:30,319 --> 00:41:34,640
He did not succeed in convincing

967
00:41:32,079 --> 00:41:36,319
everyone, but his position is more

968
00:41:34,640 --> 00:41:38,079
sophisticated than critics often

969
00:41:36,319 --> 00:41:41,440
acknowledge.

970
00:41:38,079 --> 00:41:44,720
For God, mathematical intuition was not

971
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:46,960
a mysterious sixth sense.

972
00:41:44,720 --> 00:41:50,480
It was the faculty by which we grasp

973
00:41:46,960 --> 00:41:52,880
concepts and recognize their properties.

974
00:41:50,480 --> 00:41:56,079
When a mathematician understands what a

975
00:41:52,880 --> 00:41:59,359
set is, when they comprehend the concept

976
00:41:56,079 --> 00:42:01,760
of continuity or infinity,

977
00:41:59,359 --> 00:42:04,880
this understanding is intuitive in good

978
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:07,440
old sense. It is not learned by rope

979
00:42:04,880 --> 00:42:09,760
memorization of definitions.

980
00:42:07,440 --> 00:42:12,240
It involves a direct intellectual

981
00:42:09,760 --> 00:42:14,720
perception of the concepts meaning

982
00:42:12,240 --> 00:42:17,200
implications.

983
00:42:14,720 --> 00:42:19,119
Good compared mathematical intuition to

984
00:42:17,200 --> 00:42:21,680
sense perception

985
00:42:19,119 --> 00:42:24,160
not because they are identical but

986
00:42:21,680 --> 00:42:26,800
because both involve direct contact with

987
00:42:24,160 --> 00:42:28,800
reality.

988
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:31,440
Just as vision gives us immediate

989
00:42:28,800 --> 00:42:33,359
awareness of physical objects,

990
00:42:31,440 --> 00:42:35,920
mathematical intuition gives us

991
00:42:33,359 --> 00:42:38,800
immediate awareness of abstract objects

992
00:42:35,920 --> 00:42:40,720
and concepts.

993
00:42:38,800 --> 00:42:43,599
Both are fallible.

994
00:42:40,720 --> 00:42:45,680
We can misperceive physical objects just

995
00:42:43,599 --> 00:42:47,440
as we can misunderstand mathematical

996
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:49,680
concepts

997
00:42:47,440 --> 00:42:53,119
but both put us in cognitive contact

998
00:42:49,680 --> 00:42:55,359
with objective reality.

999
00:42:53,119 --> 00:42:57,680
This analogy with perception runs deep

1000
00:42:55,359 --> 00:42:59,680
in God's thinking.

1001
00:42:57,680 --> 00:43:02,079
He believed that just as sense

1002
00:42:59,680 --> 00:43:04,160
perception evolved to track features of

1003
00:43:02,079 --> 00:43:06,000
the physical environment relevant to

1004
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:08,480
survival,

1005
00:43:06,000 --> 00:43:11,359
mathematical intuition evolved or

1006
00:43:08,480 --> 00:43:15,680
developed to track features of abstract

1007
00:43:11,359 --> 00:43:17,520
reality relevant to rational thought.

1008
00:43:15,680 --> 00:43:20,319
The process by which we clarify

1009
00:43:17,520 --> 00:43:22,160
mathematical concepts resembles the

1010
00:43:20,319 --> 00:43:26,160
process by which we learn to perceive

1011
00:43:22,160 --> 00:43:29,200
the physical world more accurately.

1012
00:43:26,160 --> 00:43:31,680
Critics jumped on this immediately.

1013
00:43:29,200 --> 00:43:33,760
Perception involves causal interaction

1014
00:43:31,680 --> 00:43:35,359
between physical objects and sense

1015
00:43:33,760 --> 00:43:38,079
organs.

1016
00:43:35,359 --> 00:43:40,319
Light from an object strikes the retina,

1017
00:43:38,079 --> 00:43:42,960
initiating a causal chain that produces

1018
00:43:40,319 --> 00:43:46,240
visual experience.

1019
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:48,720
But abstract objects are causally inert.

1020
00:43:46,240 --> 00:43:51,760
They exist outside space and time and

1021
00:43:48,720 --> 00:43:55,680
cannot interact with anything physical.

1022
00:43:51,760 --> 00:43:57,839
So how could we possibly perceive them?

1023
00:43:55,680 --> 00:44:00,640
This is the famous epistemological

1024
00:43:57,839 --> 00:44:02,319
problem for Platonism and it threatens

1025
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:05,119
to make mathematical knowledge

1026
00:44:02,319 --> 00:44:07,760
inexplicable.

1027
00:44:05,119 --> 00:44:10,880
Good had responses though they did not

1028
00:44:07,760 --> 00:44:13,359
fully dissolve the problem.

1029
00:44:10,880 --> 00:44:15,359
First he questioned whether causal

1030
00:44:13,359 --> 00:44:17,200
interaction is really necessary for

1031
00:44:15,359 --> 00:44:19,200
knowledge.

1032
00:44:17,200 --> 00:44:22,240
Why assume that all knowledge must

1033
00:44:19,200 --> 00:44:24,240
result from causal processes?

1034
00:44:22,240 --> 00:44:26,240
This seems to beg the question against

1035
00:44:24,240 --> 00:44:29,040
the possibility of knowing abstract

1036
00:44:26,240 --> 00:44:31,119
objects.

1037
00:44:29,040 --> 00:44:33,440
Second, he suggested that the

1038
00:44:31,119 --> 00:44:36,079
relationship between mind and abstract

1039
00:44:33,440 --> 00:44:38,960
reality might involve some form of

1040
00:44:36,079 --> 00:44:41,520
intentionality that is not causal in the

1041
00:44:38,960 --> 00:44:44,319
ordinary sense, but still allows

1042
00:44:41,520 --> 00:44:45,839
cognitive access.

1043
00:44:44,319 --> 00:44:47,599
This connects to his interest in

1044
00:44:45,839 --> 00:44:49,520
phenomenology

1045
00:44:47,599 --> 00:44:53,200
which studies how consciousness is

1046
00:44:49,520 --> 00:44:56,400
directed toward objects.

1047
00:44:53,200 --> 00:44:58,480
Third, and perhaps most importantly,

1048
00:44:56,400 --> 00:45:00,400
God thought the problem of mathematical

1049
00:44:58,480 --> 00:45:03,280
knowledge was no more mysterious than

1050
00:45:00,400 --> 00:45:05,359
the problem of any knowledge.

1051
00:45:03,280 --> 00:45:07,680
Even knowledge of physical objects is

1052
00:45:05,359 --> 00:45:10,079
philosophically puzzling.

1053
00:45:07,680 --> 00:45:13,520
How do patterns of neural activity give

1054
00:45:10,079 --> 00:45:15,599
rise to awareness of an external world?

1055
00:45:13,520 --> 00:45:18,560
How does subjective experience connect

1056
00:45:15,599 --> 00:45:20,960
to objective reality?

1057
00:45:18,560 --> 00:45:23,920
These questions apply to all knowledge,

1058
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:26,560
not just mathematical knowledge.

1059
00:45:23,920 --> 00:45:29,119
If we cannot solve them in general, it

1060
00:45:26,560 --> 00:45:31,280
is no special objection to Pltonism that

1061
00:45:29,119 --> 00:45:33,680
mathematical knowledge faces similar

1062
00:45:31,280 --> 00:45:35,839
difficulties.

1063
00:45:33,680 --> 00:45:38,800
Still, many philosophers found Girdle's

1064
00:45:35,839 --> 00:45:40,480
appeals to intuition unsatisfying.

1065
00:45:38,800 --> 00:45:42,880
Without a clearer account of how

1066
00:45:40,480 --> 00:45:45,520
intuition works, how we can distinguish

1067
00:45:42,880 --> 00:45:48,480
genuine intuition from error, and what

1068
00:45:45,520 --> 00:45:50,480
justifies trusting intuitive judgments,

1069
00:45:48,480 --> 00:45:52,480
the notion seems to leave mathematical

1070
00:45:50,480 --> 00:45:54,720
knowledge resting on a mysterious

1071
00:45:52,480 --> 00:45:57,280
foundation.

1072
00:45:54,720 --> 00:45:58,640
This criticism had force, and Girdle

1073
00:45:57,280 --> 00:46:01,040
struggled with it throughout his

1074
00:45:58,640 --> 00:46:03,680
philosophical career.

1075
00:46:01,040 --> 00:46:05,280
One important clarification, Girdle

1076
00:46:03,680 --> 00:46:06,800
distinguished different types of

1077
00:46:05,280 --> 00:46:09,040
intuition.

1078
00:46:06,800 --> 00:46:11,760
There is intuition of individual objects

1079
00:46:09,040 --> 00:46:14,880
or simple facts and there is intuition

1080
00:46:11,760 --> 00:46:17,599
of concepts and their relationships.

1081
00:46:14,880 --> 00:46:19,920
The latter is more fundamental. We do

1082
00:46:17,599 --> 00:46:23,359
not intuitit particular numbers one by

1083
00:46:19,920 --> 00:46:25,520
one. We grasp the concept of number and

1084
00:46:23,359 --> 00:46:27,440
from that concept we can reason about

1085
00:46:25,520 --> 00:46:29,839
all numbers.

1086
00:46:27,440 --> 00:46:32,640
This conceptual intuition is what allows

1087
00:46:29,839 --> 00:46:34,960
mathematics to be systematic rather than

1088
00:46:32,640 --> 00:46:37,119
just a collection of isolated intuitive

1089
00:46:34,960 --> 00:46:39,040
judgments.

1090
00:46:37,119 --> 00:46:41,440
Girdle also believed mathematical

1091
00:46:39,040 --> 00:46:44,400
intuition could be improved and refined

1092
00:46:41,440 --> 00:46:46,960
through intellectual work. It is not a

1093
00:46:44,400 --> 00:46:50,800
fixed faculty that some people have and

1094
00:46:46,960 --> 00:46:53,359
others lack. By studying mathematics,

1095
00:46:50,800 --> 00:46:56,160
thinking carefully about concepts, and

1096
00:46:53,359 --> 00:46:58,079
working through proofs, mathematicians

1097
00:46:56,160 --> 00:47:00,480
sharpen their intuitive grasp of

1098
00:46:58,079 --> 00:47:03,520
mathematical reality.

1099
00:47:00,480 --> 00:47:06,079
In this sense, intuition is not prior to

1100
00:47:03,520 --> 00:47:08,560
mathematical reasoning but develops

1101
00:47:06,079 --> 00:47:10,880
through it.

1102
00:47:08,560 --> 00:47:12,560
This raises questions about mathematical

1103
00:47:10,880 --> 00:47:15,920
practice that Girdle addressed

1104
00:47:12,560 --> 00:47:18,240
throughout his work. If mathematics is

1105
00:47:15,920 --> 00:47:22,240
about discovering truths in an objective

1106
00:47:18,240 --> 00:47:25,839
realm, what role do axioms play? How do

1107
00:47:22,240 --> 00:47:27,839
we justify adopting new axioms?

1108
00:47:25,839 --> 00:47:31,440
And what happens when different axiom

1109
00:47:27,839 --> 00:47:33,760
systems are incompatible?

1110
00:47:31,440 --> 00:47:36,079
Girdle's view was that axioms should be

1111
00:47:33,760 --> 00:47:38,720
evident. They should be statements that

1112
00:47:36,079 --> 00:47:41,760
we recognize as true when we clearly

1113
00:47:38,720 --> 00:47:44,319
grasp the relevant concepts.

1114
00:47:41,760 --> 00:47:46,079
The axioms of set theory, for example,

1115
00:47:44,319 --> 00:47:49,280
should capture what we intuitively

1116
00:47:46,079 --> 00:47:51,839
understand sets to be. When we say that

1117
00:47:49,280 --> 00:47:54,720
for any property, there's a set of all

1118
00:47:51,839 --> 00:47:57,200
things with that property. This seems

1119
00:47:54,720 --> 00:47:59,520
evident from the concept of set as

1120
00:47:57,200 --> 00:48:01,520
collection.

1121
00:47:59,520 --> 00:48:05,680
When we later discover this leads to

1122
00:48:01,520 --> 00:48:08,240
paradox, we revise our understanding.

1123
00:48:05,680 --> 00:48:10,400
Perhaps unrestricted comprehension was

1124
00:48:08,240 --> 00:48:12,480
not part of the true concept of set

1125
00:48:10,400 --> 00:48:14,800
after all.

1126
00:48:12,480 --> 00:48:17,920
This means axioms are not arbitrary

1127
00:48:14,800 --> 00:48:19,839
starting points chosen for convenience.

1128
00:48:17,920 --> 00:48:21,920
They are attempts to codify our

1129
00:48:19,839 --> 00:48:24,240
intuitive grasp of mathematical

1130
00:48:21,920 --> 00:48:26,720
structures.

1131
00:48:24,240 --> 00:48:28,800
Good axioms are those that accurately

1132
00:48:26,720 --> 00:48:30,480
describe the domain. they are meant to

1133
00:48:28,800 --> 00:48:33,200
formalize.

1134
00:48:30,480 --> 00:48:35,599
Bad axioms are those that misrepresent

1135
00:48:33,200 --> 00:48:37,760
or incompletely capture the intended

1136
00:48:35,599 --> 00:48:40,720
structures.

1137
00:48:37,760 --> 00:48:42,720
But here's where things get complicated.

1138
00:48:40,720 --> 00:48:46,400
Girdle believed there were mathematical

1139
00:48:42,720 --> 00:48:48,960
facts not settled by current axioms.

1140
00:48:46,400 --> 00:48:51,920
The continuum hypothesis, for instance,

1141
00:48:48,960 --> 00:48:53,839
is independent of standard set theory.

1142
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:56,880
Yet, Girdle thought it has a determinant

1143
00:48:53,839 --> 00:48:58,880
truth value. Either there is a size of

1144
00:48:56,880 --> 00:49:02,319
infinity between the natural numbers and

1145
00:48:58,880 --> 00:49:04,240
the real numbers or there is not.

1146
00:49:02,319 --> 00:49:07,040
This is an objective fact about the

1147
00:49:04,240 --> 00:49:10,480
mathematical universe even though our

1148
00:49:07,040 --> 00:49:13,680
current axioms do not decide it.

1149
00:49:10,480 --> 00:49:15,839
How can we discover such facts?

1150
00:49:13,680 --> 00:49:17,520
Girdle's answer was that we need new

1151
00:49:15,839 --> 00:49:20,160
axioms,

1152
00:49:17,520 --> 00:49:22,640
not arbitrary additions to the system,

1153
00:49:20,160 --> 00:49:25,359
but axioms that capture further aspects

1154
00:49:22,640 --> 00:49:27,680
of mathematical reality that our current

1155
00:49:25,359 --> 00:49:30,000
axioms miss.

1156
00:49:27,680 --> 00:49:33,200
He believed such axioms would eventually

1157
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:35,520
be found through conceptual analysis

1158
00:49:33,200 --> 00:49:38,800
by clarifying our understanding of the

1159
00:49:35,520 --> 00:49:42,240
concept of set and recognizing what that

1160
00:49:38,800 --> 00:49:44,160
concept implies.

1161
00:49:42,240 --> 00:49:47,040
This connects to his work on large

1162
00:49:44,160 --> 00:49:49,200
cardinal axioms which we will explore in

1163
00:49:47,040 --> 00:49:52,880
the next part.

1164
00:49:49,200 --> 00:49:55,119
For now, note the philosophical picture.

1165
00:49:52,880 --> 00:49:58,960
Mathematics proceeds by an iterative

1166
00:49:55,119 --> 00:50:01,760
process of intuition, axiomatization,

1167
00:49:58,960 --> 00:50:04,000
and conceptual refinement.

1168
00:50:01,760 --> 00:50:05,680
We grasp mathematical concepts

1169
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:07,839
intuitively.

1170
00:50:05,680 --> 00:50:09,599
We formalize this understanding in

1171
00:50:07,839 --> 00:50:11,680
axioms.

1172
00:50:09,599 --> 00:50:13,680
we discover the implications of those

1173
00:50:11,680 --> 00:50:15,760
axioms.

1174
00:50:13,680 --> 00:50:18,800
Sometimes we find our axioms were

1175
00:50:15,760 --> 00:50:21,280
inadequate or led to contradictions, so

1176
00:50:18,800 --> 00:50:23,280
we revise them.

1177
00:50:21,280 --> 00:50:25,920
Sometimes we find our axioms do not

1178
00:50:23,280 --> 00:50:27,839
settle all questions, so we add new

1179
00:50:25,920 --> 00:50:30,000
ones.

1180
00:50:27,839 --> 00:50:32,319
Throughout this process, we are trying

1181
00:50:30,000 --> 00:50:34,559
to align our formal systems with the

1182
00:50:32,319 --> 00:50:37,040
objective mathematical reality we

1183
00:50:34,559 --> 00:50:38,640
intuitit.

1184
00:50:37,040 --> 00:50:41,040
Let me turn now to how Girdle's

1185
00:50:38,640 --> 00:50:43,760
Platonism differs from other forms of

1186
00:50:41,040 --> 00:50:46,000
mathematical realism.

1187
00:50:43,760 --> 00:50:48,800
The history of philosophy contains many

1188
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:52,240
varieties of Plonism and Girdle's

1189
00:50:48,800 --> 00:50:55,040
version has distinctive features.

1190
00:50:52,240 --> 00:50:58,400
Classical Platonism deriving from Plato

1191
00:50:55,040 --> 00:51:01,280
himself posits a realm of perfect forms

1192
00:50:58,400 --> 00:51:04,480
or ideas that physical reality

1193
00:51:01,280 --> 00:51:06,559
imperfectly instantiates.

1194
00:51:04,480 --> 00:51:08,079
Mathematical objects are among these

1195
00:51:06,559 --> 00:51:10,640
forms.

1196
00:51:08,079 --> 00:51:13,520
The number three exists eternally and

1197
00:51:10,640 --> 00:51:15,599
perfectly in the realm of forms, while

1198
00:51:13,520 --> 00:51:20,079
groups of three physical objects are

1199
00:51:15,599 --> 00:51:22,240
imperfect manifestations of this ideal.

1200
00:51:20,079 --> 00:51:25,280
Knowledge of mathematical objects comes

1201
00:51:22,240 --> 00:51:28,480
through rational insight that transcends

1202
00:51:25,280 --> 00:51:30,400
sensory experience.

1203
00:51:28,480 --> 00:51:32,400
Girdle's Platonism shares the basic

1204
00:51:30,400 --> 00:51:34,960
commitment to abstract objects existing

1205
00:51:32,400 --> 00:51:36,800
independently of minds. but it is less

1206
00:51:34,960 --> 00:51:38,400
mystical and more focused on classical

1207
00:51:36,800 --> 00:51:40,240
platonism.

1208
00:51:38,400 --> 00:51:42,240
He was not particularly interested in

1209
00:51:40,240 --> 00:51:44,640
the relationship between abstract forms

1210
00:51:42,240 --> 00:51:46,319
and physical instantiations.

1211
00:51:44,640 --> 00:51:47,920
His concern was with how we gain

1212
00:51:46,319 --> 00:51:50,000
knowledge of mathematical structures

1213
00:51:47,920 --> 00:51:52,319
through conceptual analysis and rational

1214
00:51:50,000 --> 00:51:55,040
intuition.

1215
00:51:52,319 --> 00:51:57,680
Fraga and Russell, early 20th century

1216
00:51:55,040 --> 00:51:59,440
figures who influenced Girdle, developed

1217
00:51:57,680 --> 00:52:01,920
versions of logicism that combined

1218
00:51:59,440 --> 00:52:03,920
Pltonism with the idea that mathematics

1219
00:52:01,920 --> 00:52:06,000
reduces to logic.

1220
00:52:03,920 --> 00:52:08,160
They believed mathematical truths are

1221
00:52:06,000 --> 00:52:10,079
ultimately logical truths and

1222
00:52:08,160 --> 00:52:11,680
mathematical objects are logical

1223
00:52:10,079 --> 00:52:14,240
objects.

1224
00:52:11,680 --> 00:52:16,559
Girdle rejected this reduction.

1225
00:52:14,240 --> 00:52:18,640
Mathematics cannot be reduced to logic

1226
00:52:16,559 --> 00:52:21,520
because mathematical concepts like set

1227
00:52:18,640 --> 00:52:23,520
or number are not definable purely in

1228
00:52:21,520 --> 00:52:25,839
logical terms.

1229
00:52:23,520 --> 00:52:28,400
Mathematics has its own subject matter

1230
00:52:25,839 --> 00:52:31,280
distinct from logic. Even though the two

1231
00:52:28,400 --> 00:52:33,119
are closely related,

1232
00:52:31,280 --> 00:52:36,319
more recent versions of mathematical

1233
00:52:33,119 --> 00:52:38,559
realism developed after Girdle include

1234
00:52:36,319 --> 00:52:40,640
structural realism, which holds that

1235
00:52:38,559 --> 00:52:42,640
mathematical objects are positions in

1236
00:52:40,640 --> 00:52:44,240
structures rather than independent

1237
00:52:42,640 --> 00:52:46,240
entities.

1238
00:52:44,240 --> 00:52:48,720
What matters is not the intrinsic nature

1239
00:52:46,240 --> 00:52:51,839
of the number three, but its position in

1240
00:52:48,720 --> 00:52:53,200
the structure of natural numbers.

1241
00:52:51,839 --> 00:52:56,400
Girdle would likely have found this

1242
00:52:53,200 --> 00:52:58,319
congenial, but incomplete.

1243
00:52:56,400 --> 00:53:00,880
Structures are themselves abstract

1244
00:52:58,319 --> 00:53:04,720
entities that need to exist objectively

1245
00:53:00,880 --> 00:53:06,880
if realism is to be maintained.

1246
00:53:04,720 --> 00:53:09,760
Another form of realism is mathematical

1247
00:53:06,880 --> 00:53:12,400
naturalism which tries to make plonism

1248
00:53:09,760 --> 00:53:14,160
compatible with naturalism by explaining

1249
00:53:12,400 --> 00:53:18,000
mathematical knowledge in terms of

1250
00:53:14,160 --> 00:53:20,079
natural cognitive processes. Perhaps our

1251
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:22,079
brains are wired through evolution to

1252
00:53:20,079 --> 00:53:24,000
track abstract structures that happen to

1253
00:53:22,079 --> 00:53:25,760
be useful for navigating the physical

1254
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:28,240
world.

1255
00:53:25,760 --> 00:53:30,160
Girdle would have been skeptical.

1256
00:53:28,240 --> 00:53:31,920
He did not think mathematical knowledge

1257
00:53:30,160 --> 00:53:34,240
could be explained naturalistically

1258
00:53:31,920 --> 00:53:36,480
without remainder.

1259
00:53:34,240 --> 00:53:38,400
The fact that we can grasp infinitary

1260
00:53:36,480 --> 00:53:41,040
concepts like the set of all natural

1261
00:53:38,400 --> 00:53:43,839
numbers, which vastly exceeds anything

1262
00:53:41,040 --> 00:53:46,079
we encounter in physical experience,

1263
00:53:43,839 --> 00:53:48,240
suggests our mathematical capacities

1264
00:53:46,079 --> 00:53:51,040
transcend what naturalistic evolution

1265
00:53:48,240 --> 00:53:53,040
would produce. Now, I must address the

1266
00:53:51,040 --> 00:53:56,400
most common criticism of mathematical

1267
00:53:53,040 --> 00:53:58,800
platonism, the epistemological problem I

1268
00:53:56,400 --> 00:54:01,280
mentioned earlier.

1269
00:53:58,800 --> 00:54:03,680
If mathematical objects exist outside

1270
00:54:01,280 --> 00:54:06,160
space and time and cannot interact

1271
00:54:03,680 --> 00:54:08,960
causally with anything, how is

1272
00:54:06,160 --> 00:54:10,640
mathematical knowledge possible?

1273
00:54:08,960 --> 00:54:13,440
This objection has been pressed by

1274
00:54:10,640 --> 00:54:15,359
nominalists and empiricists repeatedly

1275
00:54:13,440 --> 00:54:18,720
and it remains the main reason many

1276
00:54:15,359 --> 00:54:21,520
philosophers reject platonism.

1277
00:54:18,720 --> 00:54:24,079
Paul Beniseraf formulated an influential

1278
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:25,680
version of this objection in a 1973

1279
00:54:24,079 --> 00:54:27,920
paper.

1280
00:54:25,680 --> 00:54:30,480
He argued that any adequate account of

1281
00:54:27,920 --> 00:54:32,559
mathematical knowledge must show how

1282
00:54:30,480 --> 00:54:36,079
mathematical beliefs are reliably

1283
00:54:32,559 --> 00:54:38,480
correlated with mathematical facts.

1284
00:54:36,079 --> 00:54:41,680
For ordinary knowledge, causal

1285
00:54:38,480 --> 00:54:44,000
interaction explains this correlation.

1286
00:54:41,680 --> 00:54:46,559
I believe there is a tree in front of me

1287
00:54:44,000 --> 00:54:48,720
because the tree causes certain sensory

1288
00:54:46,559 --> 00:54:50,559
experiences.

1289
00:54:48,720 --> 00:54:52,319
But abstract objects cannot cause

1290
00:54:50,559 --> 00:54:54,559
anything.

1291
00:54:52,319 --> 00:54:56,640
So what explains the correlation between

1292
00:54:54,559 --> 00:54:58,640
mathematical beliefs and mathematical

1293
00:54:56,640 --> 00:55:01,040
facts?

1294
00:54:58,640 --> 00:55:03,200
If there is no explanation,

1295
00:55:01,040 --> 00:55:06,800
platonism makes mathematical knowledge a

1296
00:55:03,200 --> 00:55:09,760
lucky accident or a mystery. Platonists

1297
00:55:06,800 --> 00:55:11,839
have offered various responses.

1298
00:55:09,760 --> 00:55:14,079
Some deny that causal interaction is

1299
00:55:11,839 --> 00:55:16,000
necessary for knowledge.

1300
00:55:14,079 --> 00:55:18,559
Perhaps there are non-causal forms of

1301
00:55:16,000 --> 00:55:22,240
epistemic access such as rational

1302
00:55:18,559 --> 00:55:23,920
insight or conceptual grasping.

1303
00:55:22,240 --> 00:55:25,680
Others argue that the correlation

1304
00:55:23,920 --> 00:55:27,520
between beliefs and facts can be

1305
00:55:25,680 --> 00:55:29,760
explained by the fact that both are

1306
00:55:27,520 --> 00:55:32,400
constrained by logic and conceptual

1307
00:55:29,760 --> 00:55:35,119
necessity.

1308
00:55:32,400 --> 00:55:37,760
We cannot coherently believe 2 + 2

1309
00:55:35,119 --> 00:55:40,160
equals 5 because that violates the

1310
00:55:37,760 --> 00:55:42,480
concept of addition.

1311
00:55:40,160 --> 00:55:45,119
The necessity built into mathematical

1312
00:55:42,480 --> 00:55:47,440
concepts ensures our beliefs align with

1313
00:55:45,119 --> 00:55:48,960
mathematical reality when we reason

1314
00:55:47,440 --> 00:55:51,680
correctly.

1315
00:55:48,960 --> 00:55:54,240
Girdle's response, as we have seen,

1316
00:55:51,680 --> 00:55:57,520
emphasized conceptual intuition and the

1317
00:55:54,240 --> 00:55:59,440
analogy with perception.

1318
00:55:57,520 --> 00:56:01,440
He did not think the epistemological

1319
00:55:59,440 --> 00:56:03,200
problem for mathematical knowledge was

1320
00:56:01,440 --> 00:56:05,119
fundamentally different from the

1321
00:56:03,200 --> 00:56:06,720
epistemological problem for any

1322
00:56:05,119 --> 00:56:08,880
knowledge.

1323
00:56:06,720 --> 00:56:11,520
All knowledge faces the question of how

1324
00:56:08,880 --> 00:56:14,960
subjective mental states can accurately

1325
00:56:11,520 --> 00:56:17,359
represent objective reality.

1326
00:56:14,960 --> 00:56:20,319
This is a deep problem in philosophy of

1327
00:56:17,359 --> 00:56:24,359
mind and epistemology, but it is not a

1328
00:56:20,319 --> 00:56:24,359
special problem for Pltonism.

1329
00:56:25,599 --> 00:56:28,640
Some contemporary philosophers have

1330
00:56:27,440 --> 00:56:30,559
developed what they call

1331
00:56:28,640 --> 00:56:33,040
indispensability arguments for

1332
00:56:30,559 --> 00:56:34,799
mathematical realism.

1333
00:56:33,040 --> 00:56:37,040
The idea is that mathematics is

1334
00:56:34,799 --> 00:56:39,839
indispensable to our best scientific

1335
00:56:37,040 --> 00:56:42,880
theories. We cannot do physics without

1336
00:56:39,839 --> 00:56:45,520
using mathematical structures. If we are

1337
00:56:42,880 --> 00:56:47,920
realists about science, if we think

1338
00:56:45,520 --> 00:56:50,960
scientific theories describe objective

1339
00:56:47,920 --> 00:56:52,880
reality, then we should be realists

1340
00:56:50,960 --> 00:56:55,200
about the mathematical structures they

1341
00:56:52,880 --> 00:56:56,880
employ.

1342
00:56:55,200 --> 00:56:59,599
This argument was not available to

1343
00:56:56,880 --> 00:57:01,440
Goodell in the same form, but he would

1344
00:56:59,599 --> 00:57:04,160
likely have endorsed it as supporting

1345
00:57:01,440 --> 00:57:06,000
his position.

1346
00:57:04,160 --> 00:57:07,680
There is one more aspect of Goodell's

1347
00:57:06,000 --> 00:57:09,200
philosophy of mathematics worth

1348
00:57:07,680 --> 00:57:11,359
exploring.

1349
00:57:09,200 --> 00:57:14,400
his views on the relationship between

1350
00:57:11,359 --> 00:57:17,920
mathematical truth and linguistic or

1351
00:57:14,400 --> 00:57:19,359
symbolic representation.

1352
00:57:17,920 --> 00:57:21,839
Some philosophers argue that

1353
00:57:19,359 --> 00:57:24,640
mathematical truth is relative to formal

1354
00:57:21,839 --> 00:57:27,520
systems or languages.

1355
00:57:24,640 --> 00:57:30,319
What counts as true in one system might

1356
00:57:27,520 --> 00:57:32,160
not be true in another.

1357
00:57:30,319 --> 00:57:34,960
This seems to follow from the existence

1358
00:57:32,160 --> 00:57:38,640
of non-standard models in logic and the

1359
00:57:34,960 --> 00:57:40,720
independence results in set theory.

1360
00:57:38,640 --> 00:57:42,559
Godell completely rejected this

1361
00:57:40,720 --> 00:57:44,480
relativism.

1362
00:57:42,559 --> 00:57:47,119
He distinguished carefully between

1363
00:57:44,480 --> 00:57:50,559
mathematical truth and provability in

1364
00:57:47,119 --> 00:57:52,160
formal systems. Truth is absolute and

1365
00:57:50,559 --> 00:57:56,160
objective.

1366
00:57:52,160 --> 00:57:58,000
Provability is relative to systems.

1367
00:57:56,160 --> 00:58:00,079
The fact that different formal systems

1368
00:57:58,000 --> 00:58:03,760
have different proving power does not

1369
00:58:00,079 --> 00:58:05,599
mean truth itself is relative. It just

1370
00:58:03,760 --> 00:58:08,000
means different systems give us access

1371
00:58:05,599 --> 00:58:10,559
to different portions of mathematical

1372
00:58:08,000 --> 00:58:13,440
reality.

1373
00:58:10,559 --> 00:58:17,119
Similarly, the existence of non-standard

1374
00:58:13,440 --> 00:58:20,079
models does not threaten objectivity.

1375
00:58:17,119 --> 00:58:22,720
When we formalize arithmetic, the formal

1376
00:58:20,079 --> 00:58:24,720
system has many models, including

1377
00:58:22,720 --> 00:58:26,880
non-standard models with strange

1378
00:58:24,720 --> 00:58:28,960
infinite numbers.

1379
00:58:26,880 --> 00:58:31,920
But this does not mean there is no fact

1380
00:58:28,960 --> 00:58:33,680
about which model is the real natural

1381
00:58:31,920 --> 00:58:36,079
numbers.

1382
00:58:33,680 --> 00:58:38,720
We intend our axioms to describe the

1383
00:58:36,079 --> 00:58:40,960
standard natural numbers and that

1384
00:58:38,720 --> 00:58:44,319
intention fixes what we are talking

1385
00:58:40,960 --> 00:58:47,640
about even if the formal system admits

1386
00:58:44,319 --> 00:58:47,640
other interpretations.

1387
00:58:47,760 --> 00:58:52,079
This points to a deep conviction in

1388
00:58:49,760 --> 00:58:54,640
Goodell's thought.

1389
00:58:52,079 --> 00:58:56,640
Meaning cannot be fully captured by

1390
00:58:54,640 --> 00:58:59,280
formal systems.

1391
00:58:56,640 --> 00:59:01,440
The meaning of mathematical concepts

1392
00:58:59,280 --> 00:59:04,079
transcends any particular

1393
00:59:01,440 --> 00:59:06,559
axiomatization.

1394
00:59:04,079 --> 00:59:08,400
We grasp the concept of natural number

1395
00:59:06,559 --> 00:59:11,040
through intuition

1396
00:59:08,400 --> 00:59:14,319
and that intuition fixes a unique

1397
00:59:11,040 --> 00:59:15,839
structure as the intended interpretation

1398
00:59:14,319 --> 00:59:19,839
even though formal systems

1399
00:59:15,839 --> 00:59:21,920
underdetermine the interpretation.

1400
00:59:19,839 --> 00:59:25,200
This is why informal mathematical

1401
00:59:21,920 --> 00:59:27,599
reasoning is indispensable.

1402
00:59:25,200 --> 00:59:30,160
We can never eliminate appeals to

1403
00:59:27,599 --> 00:59:32,960
intuitive understanding in favor of

1404
00:59:30,160 --> 00:59:35,520
purely mechanical manipulation of

1405
00:59:32,960 --> 00:59:37,839
symbols.

1406
00:59:35,520 --> 00:59:40,000
Let me conclude this part by considering

1407
00:59:37,839 --> 00:59:43,839
what hangs on the debate between

1408
00:59:40,000 --> 00:59:45,599
ploninism and its alternatives.

1409
00:59:43,839 --> 00:59:48,720
Why should anyone care whether

1410
00:59:45,599 --> 00:59:51,520
mathematical objects exist independently

1411
00:59:48,720 --> 00:59:55,359
or are human constructions?

1412
00:59:51,520 --> 00:59:58,880
What practical difference does it make?

1413
00:59:55,359 --> 01:00:02,240
For Goodell, the stakes were high. If

1414
00:59:58,880 --> 01:00:04,720
Plonism is false, then mathematics has

1415
01:00:02,240 --> 01:00:07,359
no objective truth.

1416
01:00:04,720 --> 01:00:11,119
It becomes a game we play with symbols

1417
01:00:07,359 --> 01:00:12,799
